No candidate below currently justifies beating the stated records. Three narrow questions survive, chiefly as inexpensive ways to test whether useful structure exists.

I use \(n=\lceil\log_2N\rceil\), assume distinct odd primes, and discuss balanced semiprimes unless stated otherwise. **M/A/Q** have the project’s meanings. **SIZE** denotes a representation or output-size obstruction, not a lower bound. Factoring-equivalence identifies the missing primitive; it does not prove that primitive cannot be accelerated. Literature cited below supplies the mathematical antecedent, not necessarily the proposed factoring application. Except where marked **KNOWN**, I found no established factoring use. ★ flags a surprising connection or proposed interface; priority is unverified.

**1. Knot theory / low-dimensional topology**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Arithmetic linking and higher linking.** Build \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z[1/N]\); seek linking, cup products, or Massey products distinguishing its two missing primes. | Morishita, *Knots and Primes* (2012); [“Milnor invariants and Massey products for prime numbers,” Compositio 140 (2004), 69–83](https://doi.org/10.1112/S0010437X03000137). | **RESTATEMENT / Q.** The scheme is public, but naming its individual prime boundary components requires factoring. The ordinary linking symbol is \((p/q)\). Higher symbols require specified ramified primes and additional arithmetic; the topological interpretation supplies no faster computation. Fixed-conductor abelian aggregates fall under the project’s reciprocity barrier. |
| **★ Lens-space correction-term collisions.** Construct \(L(N,a)\); evaluate \(d_i=d(L(N,a),i)\) individually by Euclidean recursion. Seek \(d_i=d_j\) with \(j\not\equiv a-1-i\pmod N\). | [Jabuka–Robins–Wang, IMRN 2013, 170–183, Thm. 1.4](https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.4397). | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK, with a factoring-hard search target.** Equality implies \(N\mid2(i-j)(i+j-a+1)\); a nonconjugate collision therefore gives a proper \(\gcd(i-j,N)\). Individual evaluation is cheap; finding the useful pair without examining \(N\) labels is the missing step. \(a=1\) supplies only trivial conjugate collisions. |
| **Quantum invariants at fixed or growing level.** Encode \(N\) by surgery; evaluate WRT/TV or Gauss-type amplitudes. | [Burton–Maria–Spreer, “Algorithms and complexity for Turaev–Viro invariants”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.04099); [Berndt–Evans–Williams, *Gauss and Jacobi Sums* (1998)](https://www.wiley.com/en-gb/gauss-and-jacobi-sums-p-9780471128076). | **AGGREGATE / Q / SIZE.** Fixed-level TV is **not generally polynomial time**: their \(r=3\) case is polynomial, \(r=4\) is #P-hard. Ordinary coprime quadratic Gauss sums give Jacobi/phase data. Level \(N\) introduces growing state spaces; explicitly representing \(\mathbb Q(\zeta_N)\) costs degree \(\varphi(N)\). Succinct numerical evaluation needs a separate algorithm and precision analysis. |
| **Torus-knot genus or Alexander polynomial.** Since \(g(T(p,q))=(p-1)(q-1)/2\), its genus would reveal \(\varphi(N)\). | Classical torus-knot formulas; arithmetic-topology background in [Morishita (2012)](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4471-2158-9). | **RESTATEMENT.** Writing \(T(p,q)\), its braid, or its Alexander polynomial already requires \(p,q\). \(T(1,N)\) is the unknot. A knot constructed from a binary multiplication circuit merely transfers the unknown solution into an invariant-computation problem. |
| **Cyclic-cover torsion as a compressed recurrence.** Fix a knot \(K\), take its \(N\)-fold branched cover, and compute \(\operatorname{Res}(\Delta_K(t),t^N-1)\). | [Silver–Williams, “Mahler measure, links and homology growth,” Topology 41 (2002), 979–991](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0003127). | **M / SIZE.** For fixed \(\Delta_K\), residues are computable through fixed-dimensional matrix powering. A gcd split comes from root orders modulo one prime. Exact torsion can have \(\Theta(N)\) bits. Selecting individual prime-index covers instead requires the factors. |

**2. Symplectic geometry / Floer theory**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Lagrangian intersections on a torus.** Choose integral slopes \(u(N),v(N)\); intersection rank is \(\lvert\det(u,v)\rvert\). Try to make connected components reveal divisors. | [Polishchuk–Zaslow, “Categorical Mirror Symmetry: The Elliptic Curve” (1998)](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9801119). | **RESTATEMENT / explicit gcd.** Primitive slopes \((1,0),(1,N)\) give rank \(N\), already known. Nonprimitive winding components are computed by gcd of supplied coordinates. Encoding a hidden \(p\)-fold component requires either \(p\) or an arithmetic construction that already splits \(N\). |
| **Floer ranks of iterated torus maps.** For a fixed hyperbolic \(A\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)\), study \(HF(A^N)\), fixed points, or primitive periodic orbits. | Fel’shtyn, [“The growth rate of symplectic Floer homology”](https://preprints.ihes.fr/storage/FELSHTYN.pdf). | **M / SIZE.** Fixed-point counts reduce to \(\lvert\det(A^N-I)\rvert\); modular values use fast powering, while exact values have \(O(N)\) bits. Primitive-orbit formulas sum over divisors of \(N\). On finite torsion grids, Smith normal form exposes gcd/order tests, not a fourth mechanism. |
| **HMS and \(N\)-isogeny correspondences.** Transfer a difficult symplectic count into elliptic-curve bundles or torsion points. | [Polishchuk–Zaslow](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9801119); [Lenstra, “Factoring integers with elliptic curves,” Annals 126 (1987), 649–673](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/1987/126-3/p09). | **KNOWN: M**, or **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT count**. Arithmetic on \(E(\mathbb Z/N)\) is ECM when a group order annihilates one component. Counting all cyclic level-\(N\) subgroups produces \(\psi(N)=(p+1)(q+1)\), hence factors, but explicitly enumerates roughly \(N\) objects. HMS changes presentation, not this cost. |
| **Higher Fukaya products and theta interference.** Use polygon-count coefficients or theta constants at \(N\)-dependent parameters, rather than ranks. | [Polishchuk, “Massey and Fukaya products on elliptic curves”](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9803017). | **A / Q / unresolved evaluation.** Familiar Gaussian specializations collapse to quadratic sums; sharp real localization pays precision and lattice-search costs. General higher products are not proved covered by M/A/Q, but no explicit efficiently computable coefficient with a demonstrated factor signal emerged. The missing step is that signal, before numerical evaluation. |

**3. Homotopy theory**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Moore spectra and chromatic probes.** Form \(M_N=\operatorname{cofib}(N:S\to S)\), or compute \(K(h)^*(BC_N)\) at a chosen prime \(\ell\). | [Hopkins–Kuhn–Ravenel, JAMS 13 (2000), 553–594, §5](https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/mypapers/hkr.pdf). | **AGGREGATE / RESTATEMENT.** At fixed \(\ell\nmid N\), the relevant \(N\)-series has invertible linear coefficient; the reduced cyclic-group contribution disappears. At \(\ell\mid N\), the probe has already selected a divisor. Replacing the coefficient field by “characteristic \(N\)” does not define Morava K-theory at a composite prime. |
| **Image of \(J\), Bernoulli denominators.** Compute \(\gcd(N,\operatorname{den}(B_{2k}/4k))\), perhaps over many \(k\). | [Adams, “On the groups \(J(X)\). IV”](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511662584.023). | **M.** The denominator’s prime support is governed by divisibilities such as \(r-1\mid2k\). Choosing many small \(k\) is a structured group-order covering strategy. Small degrees only detect small primes; reaching a hidden large prime requires covering its \(r-1\) arithmetic. Stable stems supply no free denominator oracle at huge degree. |
| **TMF with level \(N\), or fixed-level Hecke coefficients at index \(N\).** Seek ranks, Euler characteristics, or \(a_N\). | [Meier, “Topological modular forms with level structure”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.06709); [Bach–Charles, “The hardness of computing an eigenform” (2007)](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT / SIZE**, with qualifications. Level-\(N\) ranks inherit \(\varphi,\psi\)-type counts and growing modular-curve models. Bach–Charles recover a density-one set of RSA moduli from a general non-CM coefficient oracle using **both \(a_N\) and \(a_{N^2}\)**. Their theorem does not settle one isolated \(a_N\), matching the project’s remaining trace question. |
| **★ Algebraic K-theory of the public ring \(R=\mathbb Z/N\).** Compute orders of \(K_{2j-1}(R)\). | [Quillen, Annals 96 (1972), 552–586](https://doi.org/10.2307/1970825). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT / M.** CRT gives order \((p^j-1)(q^j-1)\). Already \(j=1\) gives \(\varphi(N)\); higher \(j\) give \(p^j+q^j\), hence \(p+q\). Quillen’s formula takes finite fields as input. Applying it separately to the unknown field components is exactly the unavailable operation. |

**4. Category theory / higher categories**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Split the tensor unit of \(\operatorname{Perf}(\mathbb Z/N)\).** Extract its two CRT summands through categorical idempotent completion. | [Seidel, *Fukaya Categories and Picard–Lefschetz Theory* (2008), chapter on idempotents](https://ems.press/books/zlam/2/contents). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT.** A nontrivial projector on the unit is \(e\in R\) with \(e^2=e\), \(e\ne0,1\); \(\gcd(e,N)\) factors. Knowing \(K_0(R)\cong\mathbb Z^2\) under the semiprime promise gives no projector. “Split-closed” is an existence property, not an efficient splitting algorithm. |
| **Rational representations of \(C_N\).** Present \(\mathbb Q[C_N]=\mathbb Q[x]/(x^N-1)\) sparsely; recover simple-object dimensions. | Cyclotomic decomposition; [Lenstra, “Finding small degree factors of lacunary polynomials” (1999), 267–276](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~lenstrahw/PUBLICATIONS/1999a/art.pdf). | **RESTATEMENT / SIZE.** There are \(\tau(N)=4\) rational simples, so rank alone is useless. Their dimensions include \(p-1,q-1\). Existing lacunary algorithms are polynomial in the requested factor-degree bound, which here is factor-sized; they do not recover enormous-degree cyclotomic components in polylogarithmic time. |
| **★ Weighted categorical counts.** Count all subobjects of \(R^2\), or weight each subgroup of \(C_N\) by its cardinality. | Subobject-counting/Hall viewpoint; subgroup and Grothendieck-ring framework in [Hopkins–Kuhn–Ravenel, §3](https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/mypapers/hkr.pdf). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT.** Elementary CRT gives \(\#\{U\le R^2\}=(p+3)(q+3)=N+3(p+q)+9\). Weighted cyclic-subgroup counting gives \(\sigma(N)\). These are compact definitions of powerful counts; enumeration or CRT evaluation still costs factor-sized work or requires factors. |
| **Pointed fusion/modular categories.** Build \(\mathrm{Vec}_{C_N}\), its center, or a cyclic metric category; seek prime tensor factors or modular-data signatures. | [Etingof–Nikshych–Ostrik, “On fusion categories,” Annals 162 (2005), 581–642](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0203060). | **RESTATEMENT / AGGREGATE / Q.** Rank and global dimension give \(N\) or \(N^2\). Recovering the CRT tensor factors requires a subgroup of order \(p\) or \(q\). Standard quadratic modular-data sums yield Gauss/Jacobi information; full matrices have size at least \(N\). Abstract classification does not construct the hidden subcategory. |

**5. Extremal combinatorics / Ramsey / matroids**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Matroids over \(R=\mathbb Z/N\).** Construct a small public matrix; detect different ranks or dependencies in the two residue fields. | [Fink–Moci, “Matroids over a ring,” JEMS 18 (2016), 681–731](https://doi.org/10.4171/jems/600). | **Generic-ring barrier.** A minor vanishing modulo exactly one factor immediately yields a gcd split. Determinants and Smith invariants of explicitly small integer matrices are computable already. The unresolved operation is finding a useful minor in a succinct huge matrix; declaring the two local matroids different does not find it. |
| **Ramsey witnesses in a Jacobi-colored graph.** Color \(x,y\) by \((x-y\mid N)\); seek a large homogeneous set whose differences share a hidden factor. | [Komargodski–Naor–Yogev, “White-Box vs. Black-Box Complexity of Search Problems,” ECCC TR17-015](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2017/015/). | **Q / witness-search barrier.** Coloring is cheap, but Ramsey homogeneity does not imply any nonunit difference. A difference divisible by one factor would already split. The literature explicitly distinguishes guaranteed witnesses from efficiently finding them in succinct graphs; no reduction makes an arbitrary homogeneous witness useful here. |
| **Polynomial-method certificates via binomial coefficients.** Test \(\gcd(\binom Nk,N)\), exploiting characteristic-dependent cancellations. | Lucas/Kummer arithmetic; algorithmic comparison: [Bernstein, “Integer factorization,” §14](https://www.mat.uniroma2.it/~eal/bernstein0.pdf). | **KNOWN arithmetic in new language / SIZE.** For \(1\le k<p\), both \(p,q\) divide \(\binom Nk\), so the gcd is \(N\). At \(k=p<q\), it is \(q\). Thus the useful cancellation occurs at the unknown factor scale. Accelerated product evaluation returns to factorial/product-tree factoring methods. |
| **★ Nearly regular binary matroids in NFS relations.** Build the actual relation matrix \(A\); delete a small exceptional set so the remaining column matroid has a Seymour decomposition. | [Seymour, “Decomposition of regular matroids,” JCTB 28 (1980), 305–359](https://doi.org/10.1016/0095-8956(80)90075-1). | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK: primitive replacement.** Graphic/cographic pieces admit combinatorial elimination. Ordinary large-prime graph reductions already exploit a special case. Unknown: whether the filtered matrix retains a small exceptional set, and whether finding/reconstructing the decomposition costs less than sparse linear algebra. Random-looking cores may kill the proposal. |

**6. Spectral graph theory / expanders**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **★ Spectrum of the unitary Cayley graph.** On \(\mathbb Z/N\), connect \(x,y\) when \(\gcd(x-y,N)=1\). Recover eigenvalues or multiplicities. | [Klotz–Sander, “Some properties of unitary Cayley graphs,” EJC 14 (2007), R45](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=05c60878d2b37eb23e13d8b4bf083caff98aa779&repid=rep1&type=pdf). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT; local-estimation obstruction.** CRT gives \(K_p\otimes K_q\), with eigenvalues \(\varphi(N),-(p-1),-(q-1),1\). Exact spectral access factors. However, the exceptional spectral mass is only \(O(N^{-1/2})\); ordinary sampling needs factor-sized effort to encounter it. Exact degree is already \(\varphi(N)\). |
| **\(SL_2(\mathbb Z/N)\) and LPS-style graphs.** Use fixed generators, mixing, or representation multiplicities to recover CRT components. | [Lubotzky–Phillips–Sarnak, Combinatorica 8 (1988), 261–277](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02126799); [Bourgain–Gamburd, Annals 167 (2008), 625–642](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2008/167-2/p07). | **SIZE / generic-ring barrier.** Neighbor computation is cheap; listing the group or its spectrum is not. Short fixed-word tests agree across components until a polynomial identity holds in one, producing a gcd. Expansion proves mixing, not recovery of CRT coordinates or efficient estimation of tiny spectral multiplicities. |
| **Expander navigation in \((\mathbb Z/N)^*\).** Walk using small factor-base generators and find short paths to prescribed residues. | Expansion/sieve antecedents: [Bourgain–Gamburd–Sarnak, *Affine linear sieve, expanders and sum-product* (2010)](https://www.math.ias.edu/files/Bourgain_Gamburd_Sarnak_Affine.pdf). | **M / project’s navigation–DL barrier.** Mixing supplies random endpoints. Producing a short factor-base word for a specified endpoint is the hard primitive: it constructs relations and, in appropriate prime-modulus formulations, solves discrete logs. Existence of short paths supplies no navigation algorithm. |
| **Spectral preconditioners for NFS linear algebra.** Apply Laplacian solvers or sparsification to the relation incidence graph. | [Spielman–Teng, SIMAX 35 (2014), 835–885](https://doi.org/10.1137/090771430); [Montgomery, EUROCRYPT 1995, 106–120](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-49264-X_9). | **KNOWN baseline; characteristic barrier.** Approximate real SDD solving does not preserve exact \(\mathbb F_2\) dependencies. Even \(\ker(A^\top A)\) can strictly exceed \(\ker A\) in characteristic two. A useful transfer must preserve parity and reconstruct exact dependencies; no such general spectral transfer is supplied by expansion. |

**7. Tropical geometry**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Tropicalize \(xy=N\) at finite places.** Read \(v_\ell(x)+v_\ell(y)=v_\ell(N)\), or Newton polygons of \(N\)-dependent polynomials. | [Maclagan–Sturmfels, *Introduction to Tropical Geometry* (2015)](https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/161); [Guàrdia–Montes–Nart, higher-order Newton polygons](https://upcommons.upc.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/d0cbc387-9886-4c36-96e7-43a7add44dcd/content). | **RESTATEMENT.** A valuation at a supplied prime is efficiently computable. Finding a place where \(v_\ell(N)>0\) finds a factor. Algorithms over \(\mathbb Q_\ell\) assume \(\ell\) is supplied; there is no single field \(\mathbb Q_N\) combining unknown prime valuations. |
| **Archimedean amoeba / tropical balancing.** Use \(\log x+\log y=\log N\), then seek integral lifts near the balanced point. | [Maclagan–Sturmfels](https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/161). | **A.** The tropical line knows the product and scale, not integrality of its coordinates. Recovering a nontrivial integral lift is factoring. Rounding or refining near \(\sqrt N\) becomes Fermat/lattice approximation, with the familiar search and known-bits costs. |
| **Tropical norm optimization instead of sieving.** Partition NFS parameter space into cells with favorable valuations and small norms; enumerate promising cells. | [Murphy–Brent, CATS 1998, 199–213](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/~brent/pub/pub178.html); [Gao–Lauder, “Decomposition of polytopes and polynomials”](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0012099). | **KNOWN preprocessing / size–entropy and resultant barriers.** Small-prime valuation optimization is already polynomial selection and sieving. Newton polytopes control support and degree, not simultaneous smoothness of integer values. To improve the asymptotic bound, cells must provide a demonstrably cheaper relation constructor; convex optimization alone does not provide one. |

**8. Discrete geometry / sphere packing / tilings**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Extract the \(N\)-th coefficient of the \(E_8\) theta series.** Its shell count is \(r_{E_8}(2N)=240\sigma_3(N)\). | [Viazovska, Annals 185 (2017), 991–1015](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2017/185-3/p07); modular-form coefficient hardness in [Bach–Charles](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT.** Subtracting \(N^3+1\) from \(\sigma_3(N)\) gives \(p^3+q^3\), hence \(p+q\). The theta identity is not a fast isolated-coefficient algorithm. Viazovska’s Fourier inequalities certify optimal density; they do not extract a distant coefficient in polylogarithmic time. |
| **A given explicit tile \(A\subset C_N\).** Decide whether \(A\oplus B=C_N\), or construct \(B\). | [Coven–Meyerowitz, J. Algebra 212 (1999), 161–174](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9802122). | **RESTATEMENT / SIZE.** Necessarily \(\lvert A\rvert\lvert B\rvert=N\). Any proper nontrivial tile therefore has \(\lvert A\rvert=p\) or \(q\): its cardinality already factors \(N\), and its explicit description has factor-sized length. Under the balanced promise, a polylog-sized explicit tile is either a singleton or cannot tile. |
| **Non-Hajós tilings and counting complements.** Ask for both factors aperiodic, or count complements of a supplied tile. | [de Bruijn (1953)](https://research.tue.nl/en/publications/on-the-factorization-of-finite-abelian-groups/); [Kolountzakis–Matolcsi, “Algorithms for translational tiling”](https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.4338). | **Constant answer / representation barrier.** \(C_{pq}\) has the Hajós property: both factors cannot be aperiodic. One aperiodic factor alone is allowed. Decision/counting is computable without factoring by finite exhaustive search, but no polylog bound follows. Their CM tests are polynomial in **diameter**, not binary encoding length. For succinct tiles, even cardinality may be hard; a factoring reduction must be supplied separately. |
| **Planar dimers or bounded-width tiling strips.** Construct a graph from \(N\), compute its Pfaffian, or evaluate \(\operatorname{tr}(T^N)\). | [Fisher, Phys. Rev. 124 (1961), 1664](https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.124.1664); [Kenyon–Okounkov–Sheffield, Annals 163 (2006), 1019–1056](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2006/163-3/p06). | **SIZE / AGGREGATE.** Pfaffian computation is polynomial in the explicit graph size, which may be \(N\). Fixed-width transfer matrices give fast modular counts, but modulo fixed \(m\) these are eventually periodic in \(N\). Exact counts generally have \(\Theta(N)\) bits. No factor-sensitive feature follows merely from tractability. |
| **★ Periodic honeycomb determinants as generalized Wendt resultants.** Define \(D_N(c)=\operatorname{Res}_X(X^N-1,(X+c)^N-1)\); compute \(D_N(c)\bmod N\) and take a gcd. This is a signed/twisted periodic dimer determinant. | Dimer framework above; [Helou, “On Wendt’s determinant,” Math. Comp. 66 (1997), 1341–1346](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-97-00870-3). | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK, weakly.** For \(N=pq\), \(D_N(c)\equiv D_q(c)\pmod p\). Thus a compressed global determinant can behave differently at the two factors. Missing independently: a useful asymmetric-divisibility rate and subexponential evaluation without degree-\(N\) expansion. It may ultimately collapse to exceptional multiplicative-order conditions. |
| **Substitution tilings and aperiodic monotiles.** Encode \(N\) in an inflation matrix or finite patch; read periods, frequencies, or completion counts. | Finite-state tiling antecedent: [Coven–Geller–Silberger–Smillie, “The Symbolic Dynamics of Tiling the Integers”](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9810024). | **RESTATEMENT / computation-simulation barrier.** Supplied finite matrices give computable eigenvalues and recurrences, without selecting hidden divisors. A patch enforcing multiplication can encode factor search, but completion must then perform that search. Aperiodicity or universality supplies no bound on patch area, assembly work, or readout time. |

**9. Logic / proof complexity**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Extract factors from bounded-arithmetic proofs.** Formalize the total factor-or-prime relation and apply witnessing. | [Krajíček–Pudlák, Information and Computation 140 (1998), 82–94](https://doi.org/10.1006/inco.1997.2674); [Jeřábek, “Integer factoring and modular square roots”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5220). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT witnessing target.** A suitable \(S^1_2\) totality proof would yield polynomial-time witnesses. Adding counting or weak-pigeonhole principles changes the available witnessing machinery; it does not automatically produce an FP algorithm. The missing step is an effective witness extractor with the required running time. |
| **Factoring CNFs and short refutations.** Build a polynomial-sized multiplication CNF \(F_N(u,v)\); use proofs for unsatisfiable prefix restrictions to recover factors. | [Krajíček–Pudlák (1998)](https://doi.org/10.1006/inco.1997.2674). | **Proof-search barrier.** Short proofs, even if available, need not be efficiently findable. A composite’s unrestricted factor CNF is satisfiable. Tautologies mentioning its actual \(p,q\) already require them. Conditional cryptographic proof lower bounds are not unconditional factoring-time lower bounds; no relevant unconditional general EF breakthrough follows here. |
| **PPA / PWPP / PPP algorithms.** Construct Jeřábek’s parity or collision instance, then solve it faster using its arithmetic structure. | [Jeřábek, Computational Complexity 25 (2016), 675–708](https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5220). | **KNOWN reductions / Q.** Factoring reduces by randomized polynomial-time reductions to PPA and to WeakPigeon, hence PWPP and PPP. GRH derandomizes the reductions. This is not an unconditional deterministic-membership statement. Finding the second parity endpoint or nontrivial square-root collision remains the hard algorithmic step. |
| **Use the 2026 ETH-refutation algorithm on factoring circuits.** Encode proper multiplication and self-reduce SAT. | OpenAI, [*The Exponential Time Hypothesis is False*, October 25, 2026 preprint](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/README.md); [quantitative contraction theorem](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/build/quantitative-reduction.tex). | **Quantitative reduction barrier.** Treating the announced result conditionally, even an optimistic \(O(n)\)-variable encoding with time \(2^{O(n/\sqrt{\log n})}\) is asymptotically much slower than NFS. Actual multiplication encodings add auxiliary variables. Refuting ETH neither proves P=NP nor yields \(L_N[\alpha<1/3]\) factoring. A much stronger bound or special arithmetic compression is required. |

**10. Cellular automata and computation models**

| Candidate: object and desired feature | Literature / existing use | Verdict and exact obstruction |
|---|---|---|
| **Compile factoring into Rule 110.** Encode \(N\) in its initial condition and inspect the eventual factor output. | [Cook, “Universality in Elementary Cellular Automata,” Complex Systems 15 (2004), 1–40](https://www.paradise.caltech.edu/cook/papers/); [Neary–Woods, ICALP 2006](https://tilde.ini.uzh.ch/users/tneary/public_html/NearyWoodsBCRI-04-06.pdf). | **KNOWN simulation, no factoring acceleration.** Polynomial simulation overhead transfers an existing algorithm. It does not shorten its computation. Binary encoding of a huge observation time is not permission to fast-forward there. P-completeness of bounded prediction concerns parallel simulation, not a factoring lower bound. |
| **Reversible modular CA dynamics.** Implement \(x\mapsto ax\bmod N\), or a reversible arithmetic circuit; recover its period or hidden invariant blocks. | [Bennett, “Logical Reversibility of Computation,” IBM JRD 17 (1973), 525–532](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall06/cos576/papers/bennett73.html). | **M.** Reversibility preserves information and can reduce dissipation. Period extraction remains multiplicative-order computation. Fast forward evaluation \(a^t\bmod N\) is already easy; recovering the unknown period from those evaluations is precisely what has not become easy classically. |
| **Self-assembly searches all factor pairs.** Let tiles represent multiplication constraints; detect a successful assembly. | [Winfree, *Algorithmic Self-Assembly of DNA*, Caltech thesis (1998)](https://thesis.caltech.edu/1866/). | **Parallel-work barrier.** Exponentially many simultaneous candidate assemblies can reduce elapsed laboratory time only by spending corresponding material/work. An honest classical comparison counts total tiles, molecules, attachment events, and readout. Compact tile rules do not make all possible assemblies a free resource. |
| **Analog/BSS root finding.** Encode \(xy=N\), periodicity, or an optimization landscape over reals; read an integer factor. | [Blum–Shub–Smale, Bull. AMS 21 (1989), 1–46](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0273-0979-1989-15750-9). | **A / model mismatch.** Real solutions to \(xy=N\) are abundant; selecting integral ones is the arithmetic difficulty. Exact comparisons, arbitrary real constants, or infinitely precise readout can hide uncharged computation. A credible transfer needs uniform constants and explicit precision, conditioning, and bit-cost bounds. |
| **Shamir’s unit-cost arithmetic RAM.** Execute the \(O(\log N)\)-operation factoring program. | [Shamir, IPL 8 (1979), 28–31](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90087-5); [MIT report and abstract](https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/5fdbc9a1-3c67-4d3c-b0d0-e40660c5f6d4). | **KNOWN, model mismatch.** The program permits enormous intermediates; arithmetic-operation count suppresses their bit lengths. Honest restrictions are polynomially bounded word lengths with ordinary implementable operations, or charging each operation by operand size, together with total memory/work. These restrictions remove the claimed polynomial bit-time consequence. |

**Ranked surviving questions**

1. **Regular-matroid structure of relation matrices — best algorithmic prospect.**  
   Let \(A\) be a filtered NFS/MNFS relation matrix with \(B\) columns. First question: does deleting \(k\le B^{1-\delta}\) columns leave a regular binary matroid with a decomposition constructible in \(B^{2-\varepsilon}\) bit operations? Charge dependency reconstruction too. Conversely, many column-disjoint Fano or dual-Fano minors would quickly reject small-deletion versions. A positive result targets the **MNFS constant**, with relation supply reoptimized; it does not automatically change exponent \(1/3\).

2. **Lens-space collision search — concrete, but probably another collision barrier.**  
   Set \(a=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor\), first taking its gcd with \(N\). Define
   \[
   C_N=\#\{i<j:\ d_i=d_j,\quad j\not\equiv a-1-i\pmod N\}.
   \]
   First lemma/question: is \(C_N>0\) for a positive proportion of balanced semiprimes, and can the collision locations be characterized through the Euclidean recursion? Only then investigate search faster than scanning the labels. This sharpens the project’s existing class-E opening; it establishes no new leak family.

3. **Periodic dimers / Wendt residues — most unusual, least supported.**  
   First establish whether a small, factor-independent set of shifts \(c\) produces useful asymmetric divisibility. The identity
   \[
   D_{pq}(c)\equiv D_q(c)\pmod p
   \]
   follows because both defining polynomials become \(p\)-th powers modulo \(p\), and resultants acquire power \(p^2\). The immediate research question is quantitative: does asymmetry occur often enough outside recognizable order exceptions? Fast evaluation is a separate obligation. The dimer interpretation is surprising; neither a new factoring mechanism nor a useful complexity bound is established.